EA = (f"Purpose: This study examines how institutional pressures (coercive, normative and mimetic) steer executive agencies towards substantive implementation or symbolic compliance with performance-based budgeting and accrual accounting, how these two responses affect financial accountability through the perceived usefulness of reported information, and whether the maturity of the financial information system conditions these relationships. "
      f"Design/methodology/approach: The study is applied and survey-based. Data were collected with a questionnaire from {n} financial managers, comptrollers, budget experts and accountants of executive agencies and analysed with covariance-based structural equation modelling using fully latent variables (no item composites), latent interaction terms and 2,000-draw bootstrap estimation of indirect and conditional indirect effects. Convergent and discriminant validity, common-method bias, competing models, DWLS estimation, residual-centred product indicators, a respecified model, outlier trimming and an experienced-respondent subsample were used as checks. "
      f"Findings: The measurement model fitted well (CFI = {R['cfa']['cfi']:.3f}; RMSEA = {R['cfa']['rmsea']:.3f}), composite reliability ranged from {min(v['cr'] for v in REL.values()):.2f} to {max(v['cr'] for v in REL.values()):.2f}, and the highest HTMT was {R['htmt_max']:.2f}. Coercive pressure increased both substantive implementation (β = {PA['SUB~COE']['beta']:.2f}) and symbolic compliance (β = {PA['SYM~COE']['beta']:.2f}); normative pressure strengthened substantive implementation (β = {PA['SUB~NOR']['beta']:.2f}) and weakened symbolic compliance (β = {PA['SYM~NOR']['beta']:.2f}); mimetic pressure increased symbolic compliance (β = {PA['SYM~MIM']['beta']:.2f}) but had no significant effect on substantive implementation. Substantive implementation raised the usefulness of information (β = {PA['USE~SUB']['beta']:.2f}) and financial accountability (β = {PA['ACC~SUB']['beta']:.2f}), whereas symbolic compliance reduced both (β = {PA['USE~SYM']['beta']:.2f} and {PA['ACC~SYM']['beta']:.2f}); usefulness, in turn, improved accountability (β = {PA['ACC~USE']['beta']:.2f}). Information-system maturity strengthened the effect of substantive implementation on usefulness (β = {PA['USE~INTS']['beta']:.2f}) and weakened the effect of coercive pressure on symbolic compliance (β = {PA['SYM~INTC']['beta']:.2f}). The total indirect effect of coercive pressure was small because its constructive and ceremonial routes largely offset each other, whereas that of normative pressure was clearly positive (indirect effect = {IN['NOR_tot_mean']['est']:.2f}, 95% CI [{IN['NOR_tot_mean']['lo']:.2f}, {IN['NOR_tot_mean']['hi']:.2f}]). Full mediation was supported, because adding direct paths from pressures to accountability did not improve fit. "
      f"Conclusions and implications: The effect of financial reforms depends less on the intensity of mandates than on the type of organisational response to them and on the information infrastructure that makes this response visible and usable. Strengthening professional norms, monitoring the use rather than the submission of reports, and investing in integrated, real-time and auditable systems are likely to be more productive than additional legal requirements alone. "
      f"Originality/value: The study separates substantive and symbolic responses to the same institutional pressures, links them to accountability through information usefulness, and identifies the conditioning role of system maturity in the Iranian public sector.")
